# Caddy — the public entry points of the FPVTP! VPS.
#
# Slice T4 of the deployment design:
# sim/docs/superpowers/specs/2026-09-07-dual-mode-deployment-design.md
#
# Installation: see deploy/README.md.
#   cp deploy/Caddyfile /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   # replace the `example.org` names with the real domain, and drop the
#   # second block if you are not standing up the optional download page
#   caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#   systemctl reload caddy
#
# Caddy obtains and renews the TLS certificates on its own (Let's Encrypt), as
# long as the names really point at the machine's IP and ports 80 and 443 are
# reachable.
#
# `fpvtp.example.org` and `updates.fpvtp.example.org` are examples: this file
# is a template, it is never deployed as it stands.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. The game server — the API and the built files. REQUIRED.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
fpvtp.example.org {
	# The Node server (fpvtp.service) only listens on 127.0.0.1:8080; this
	# block, and this block alone, exposes it. Everything goes through the
	# proxy: the API (/__operator, /__map-api) as well as the static files,
	# because sim/server/static.mjs carries things a file_server could not
	# reproduce (the dynamic /scenes.json catalogue built from the data
	# directory, the deliberate absence of an SPA fallback, the immutable
	# Cache-Control).

	# WITHOUT A CDN (Caddy is the only proxy) — keep this line.
	#
	# It drops any X-Forwarded-For the CLIENT sent, before the proxy builds its
	# own. Caddy APPENDS the real peer to whatever arrives, so without it the
	# header reads `<whatever the client claimed>, <real address>`, and only the
	# last entry is trustworthy. The server reads the last hop for exactly that
	# reason (sim/server/auth.mjs, clientIp); this makes it right for the right
	# reason rather than by luck.
	#
	# BEHIND CLOUDFLARE (or any CDN) — DELETE this line, and read on.
	#
	# Cloudflare puts the visitor's real address in the X-Forwarded-For it
	# sends, and in CF-Connecting-IP. Dropping the header here throws that away:
	# Caddy then appends Cloudflare's own edge address and the server sees every
	# visitor on earth as one client. A limit of five signups an hour becomes
	# five for the whole internet.
	#
	# Two things go together, and neither works alone:
	#
	#   1. delete the line below, and set FPVTP_TRUST_CF_IP=1 in
	#      deploy/fpvtp.service so the server reads CF-Connecting-IP;
	#   2. firewall 80 and 443 to Cloudflare's published ranges — see
	#      deploy/README.md. Without this, the origin is reachable directly and
	#      anyone can send a CF-Connecting-IP of their choosing, which is the
	#      exact hole the header was supposed to close.
	request_header -X-Forwarded-For
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080

	# SSE — READ THIS BEFORE WORRYING.
	# Acquisition job progress is streamed as `text/event-stream`
	# (sim/server/api.mjs). There is NOTHING to configure for it: Caddy's
	# reverse_proxy does not buffer responses, it flushes them as they come.
	# Do not go looking for an equivalent of `proxy_buffering off;` — that
	# directive does not exist, because it has no reason to. (The
	# `x-accel-buffering: no` header the server already sends would cover
	# nginx if the front end were ever swapped.)
	#
	# No compression (`encode`) is enabled here ON PURPOSE: its interaction
	# with the SSE stream could not be verified on this machine (Caddy is not
	# installed on it). If you add it, first check that an acquisition job
	# still shows its progress live.

	# Access logs go to journald, not to a file.
	#
	# `output file /var/log/caddy/fpvtp.log` was here and it cost a deployment.
	# The package's unit declares neither LogsDirectory= nor ReadWritePaths=, so
	# the directory has to be created by hand — and creating it correctly
	# (caddy:caddy, writable, verified with `sudo -u caddy touch`) still left the
	# service refusing to start with `open …/fpvtp.log: permission denied`. One
	# line of configuration, one platform-specific failure mode, and a checklist
	# step that has to be done between two other steps or nothing starts.
	#
	# Caddy already writes everything to stderr and systemd already captures it,
	# so nothing is lost:  journalctl -u caddy -f
	# Rotation becomes journald's problem, which is where it belongs.
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Desktop installer distribution (Electron). OPTIONAL — skip it if in doubt.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THIS BLOCK IS NO LONGER WHAT UPDATES INSTALLED APPS. Since the repository is
# public, sim/electron-builder.yml uses `provider: github`: electron-updater
# reads the GitHub Releases API directly, with no server of ours in the path.
# An installation that changes nothing needs neither this block, the
# `updates.` DNS name, nor /srv/fpvtp-updates.
#
# What it is still good for, and why it is kept:
#
#   - a download page of your own, so people are not sent to GitHub;
#   - an alternative update feed for a fork or a private deployment, pointed at
#     with FPVTP_UPDATE_URL (see sim/electron/main.js), which overrides the
#     baked feed at runtime without rebuilding the app.
#
# CHOICE: a dedicated SUBDOMAIN (`updates.`), not a path under the game's
# domain. Three reasons:
#
#   1. The game block proxies EVERYTHING to Node. Serving a path statically
#      from that same block would need a `handle_path` placed before the
#      `reverse_proxy`, hence a site whose reading depends on directive order —
#      exactly the kind of subtlety that breaks at the next edit, in a file
#      nobody rereads often.
#   2. electron-updater on the generic provider wants a base URL that is the
#      root of a directory (`latest.yml` is looked up next to the installers).
#      A site root is simpler to give, and simpler to change.
#   3. These files are public, unauthenticated, whereas the game API is not
#      (operator key). Two different access regimes: two separate blocks, and
#      the separation is visible.
#
# NO AUTHENTICATION HERE, DELIBERATELY: anyone must be able to download an
# installer and receive automatic updates.
#
# The directory is filled by deploy/deploy.sh (last step), which mirrors the
# Release assets into it.
updates.fpvtp.example.org {
	root * /srv/fpvtp-updates
	file_server {
		# A listing, so a human can pick their platform by hand without
		# knowing the exact filename.
		browse
	}

	# electron-updater reads `latest.yml` (Windows) and `latest-linux.yml`
	# (Linux) at every app start: they must be revalidated, never served from
	# a cache. The installers carry their version in their name and never
	# change under that name.
	@feeds path /latest.yml /latest-linux.yml
	header @feeds Cache-Control "no-cache"

	@installers path *.exe *.AppImage *.blockmap
	header @installers Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"

	# journald here too — see the note in the block above.
}
